= IKEA effect (simple) =

The '''IKEA effect''' is the tendency to value something more when you helped make it. A table that you assembled yourself can feel more valuable than an identical table built by somebody else.

Researchers have observed the effect when people made furniture kits, origami and LEGO objects. Finishing the task was important in some experiments. A review combining many studies found an average effect, although how large it is depends on the situation.

It is '''not''' the same as the [[Sunk cost effect/Simple|sunk cost effect]], and valuing something personally is not automatically a mistake. A handmade object can genuinely mean more to you, even if it does not work better or sell for a higher price.

== A useful check ==
When choosing between your creation and an alternative, ask three separate questions:
# What does my creation mean to me?
# Does it perform the task better?
# Would somebody who did not make it value it similarly?

Independent tests and clear performance measures can help with fair comparisons. They are reasonable precautions, not proven universal cures for this effect.

